# Chapter 5, part 1: what a marker is, the table of the markers, the panels,
# the screen and the print.  Headlines of the second level and below only.

.h2 What a marker is [markers-what]
.ix Marker, what it is
.ix Marker character
A marker is one item of the text.  On the screen it takes one cell, the
cursor stops on it, and it is selected, copied, cut, pasted and moved
together with the letters around it.  Because it is an item and not a letter,
Search does not find it and does not look inside it ({n:edit-b-search}).

A marker is not a command that is carried out while the document is edited.
It stands in the text and is carried out when the document is printed or
previewed: a Date marker holds the way to write a date, and the date itself
is made at the time of the print.  Only a few markers show their result on
the screen, and they show it as text of their own: a Counter and a
Cross-reference show the number or the tag that they print (when their
display mode is Long, which is their default), a Date, a Time and the other
values show the value in brackets, and an Equation, a Picture, a Keycap and a
LaTeX marker show an image in the X11 version.

.h3 Where a marker may stand
A marker may stand anywhere in the text of a block: in a headline, in the text
of a note, in a column of a table.  The Cross-reference marker may stand only
in a structured document, because it refers to a section; in an unstructured
document its name is in parentheses in the Marker Commands table and the
command is refused with the message Invalid marker selection (the full text is
in {n:markers-messages}).  The Page Number and Section markers stand only in a
Header or a Footer ({n:format-page-headers}); in the Marker Commands table of
the text of a document their names are in parentheses and the command is
refused in the same way.  A Page Break, a Column Break, an Orientation marker
and a Whitespace marker are best put in a block of their own, and the Keep
marker has its own rules, which are in its entry.

.h3 How a marker is made and changed
The steps are the same for every marker and are described in {x:edit-b-marker}; this is the short form.
1. Put the cursor where the marker is to be and press {k:Alt-F12} (or
   {k:Ctrl-F12}).  The table Marker Commands opens, with the names in three
   columns.
2. Move to a name with the arrow keys, or press the first letter of its name,
   and press {k:Ins} or {k:Return}.
3. If the marker has a panel it opens, with the starting values.  Fill in
   the fields and press {k:Ins}.  {k:Esc} cancels the panel and nothing is
   inserted.  A marker without a panel (Author, Column Break, Description,
   Else, Endif, Filename, Pathname, Printer and Revision) is inserted at
   once.
4. The marker is put into the text before the character that the cursor was
   on, and the cursor moves past it.
.pic pictures/markers-menu.png 5.4 The table Marker Commands, with the names of all the markers in three columns.
To change a marker, put the cursor on it and press the same key: its panel
opens with the saved values.  The markers that hold text of their own (Note,
Comment and Equation) first open an editor for that text.

.h2 The markers in one table [markers-table]
.ix Marker table
.ix Display mode defaults
.ix Where markers may stand
The table lists all the markers in the order of the table Marker Commands.
The column Screen is the display mode that the marker has until the document
says otherwise ({n:markers-screen}): Diamond, Name, Short, Long or Inline.
The column Print says what the Printer does with the marker in a Final print
and in a Preview: Text means that a text is printed, Typeset that a thing is
made on the page (a rule, a space, a picture), Label that the name of the
marker and what it holds are printed in a small box in a typewriter face, and
Nothing that nothing is printed.  The last column says what is printed; the
entries give the differences of a Draft print.
.table 14 7 6 30
Marker | Screen | Print | What is printed
Author | Long | Text | The author of the document.
Column Break | Short | Typeset | A page break.
Comment | Short | Nothing | Draft: the comment, by name and time.
Counter | Long | Text | The number, in the style of the counter.
Cross-reference | Long | Text | The tag, the headline or both of a
 section, or its page number.
Date | Long | Text | The date of the print.
Description | Long | Text | The description of the document.
Else | Name | Nothing | The text after it is printed when the If is false.
Endif | Name | Nothing | The end of the condition of an If.
Equation | Inline | Typeset | The formula, as TeX math.
Figure | Short | Label | The label with the name of the figure.
Filedate | Long | Text | The date of the file on disk.
Filename | Long | Text | The name of the file, in capitals.
Filetime | Long | Text | The time of the file on disk.
Font | Short | Typeset | The family of type to the block's end.
If | Long | Nothing | The text after it is printed when the expression
 | | | is true ({n:marker-if}).
Include | Long | Text | The text of the document that the marker
 | | | names ({n:marker-include}).
Index | Short | Label | The label with the main heading.
Keep | Long | Nothing | The blocks it encloses stay on one page.
Keycap | Inline | Typeset | A key box with the legend.
LaTeX | Inline | Typeset | The text of the marker, as it is typed
 | | | ({n:marker-latex}); in the X11 version the picture that LaTeX
 | | | makes of it ({n:marker-latex-picture}).
Line | Short | Typeset | A rule or a row of characters.
Merge | Long | Label | The label with the Variable Name.
Note | Short | Typeset | A footnote, levelnote or endnote.
Orientation | Short | Typeset | Portrait or landscape pages.
Page Break | Short | Typeset | A page, odd page or even page break.
Page Number | Name | Text | The page number, in a header or a footer.
Pathname | Long | Text | The path of the document.
Picture | Inline | Typeset | The picture; the label if it cannot be read.
Printer | Long | Text | The name of the printer of the document.
Revision | Long | Text | The revision of the document.
Section | Name | Text | The number or the title of the section, in a header or
 a footer.
Table | Short | Label | The label TABLE.
Time | Long | Text | The time of the print.
Whitespace | Short | Typeset | Vertical space.
.end
The only marker that differs in a Draft print is the Comment (printed in a
Draft only).

.h2 Panels and fields [markers-panels]
.ix Marker panel
.ix Marker fields
.ix Field kinds of marker panels
.ix Keypad + in a marker panel
Every marker that has fields opens a panel in a box that is titled with the
name of the marker.  The panel is a form: one field is highlighted, the label
of the field is in bold at its left, and the box has the line "Ins accepts,
Esc cancels" at the bottom.  The keys of the panel are in the table of
{x:edit-b-marker}; what matters for this chapter is the kinds of
field.
.table 12 48
Kind | What it holds
Text | A string.  A field that is marked "required" in the entries must not
 be empty when {k:Ins} is pressed: the message is "No string specified".
Number | A number with a fixed number of decimal places: zero (the counter
 number, the center of a line), one (the shades of a keycap) or three (lengths
 and scales).  A number is typed as digits, with a decimal point if the field
 has decimals, for example 2.5.  Anything else gives "Not a valid number
 (label)".
Choice | One entry of a list.  {k:Space}, {k:Left} and {k:Right} step
 through the list, and {k:Keypad +}, {k:Alt-+} or {k:F2} show it to pick from.
Shown only | A value that the panel shows and that you cannot change (the
 Created time of a comment, the Section Tag and Headline of a
 cross-reference).  The cursor skips it.
.end
.h3 Defaults and brackets
A field that is left at its default follows the setting of the Global menu
when that is changed later: a Date marker whose Format is left as "Month D,
Year" follows the Date Format of the Global menu.  Two kinds of default are
shown in square brackets.  The first entry of some choice lists stands for a
value that comes from the document: [Footnote] in the Type of a Note is the
Primary Note Type of Global, Marker; [Tag] in the Style of a Cross-reference;
[Portrait] in the Orientation marker; [Text] in the Font of a Font marker and
a Keycap; [Style of the counter] in a Counter.  The fields of a Keycap that
are numbers show the value of the Keycap Format in brackets, such as [0.0],
until a value is typed.  A value that is typed replaces the bracket, even if
it is equal to the global value, so that it stays when the global value
changes.

.h3 Lengths and units
Lengths are typed in the panel as numbers of the unit of the field.  The
Picture and the Whitespace marker have a field Unit with the eleven units that
TeX defines.
.table 6 54
Unit | Meaning
in | Inch.  The default.
cm | Centimeter.
mm | Millimeter.
pt | Point, one 72.27th of an inch.
bp | Big point, one 72nd of an inch.
pc | Pica, 12 points.
dd | Didot point.
cc | Cicero, 12 didot points.
sp | Scaled point, the smallest unit of TeX.
em | The width of the letter M in the current font.
ex | The height of the letter x in the current font.
.end
The Line marker's Length and Weight and the Picture's Width and Height are
given in inches in the panel; the entry of each marker says where the Unit is
used.  The decimal places of a length are three, so the smallest step is one
thousandth.

.h3 File name fields
The fields that hold the name of a file (the File Name of an Include, the
File Name of a Picture) can be filled from a list: with the cursor in the field
press {k:Keypad +}, {k:Alt-+} or {k:F2}, and a checklist of the files shows
only those that fit the marker (documents ending in {c:.doc} for an Include;
files ending in {c:.png}, {c:.jpg}, .jpeg, {c:.pdf} or .eps for a Picture).
Checklists are
described in {x:file-checklist}.

.h2 How a marker looks on the screen [markers-screen]
.ix Display mode
.ix Marker display modes
.ix Diamond, Name, Short, Long, Hidden, Inline
.ix Display Hidden Markers
Each type of marker has a display mode, chosen on the panels Marker Display
Modes of Global, Marker ({n:format-marker}).  The mode is kept with the
document, and changing it marks the document as changed.
.table 7 53
Mode | The marker is drawn as
Diamond | One diamond character, whatever the marker is.
Name | The short name of the type in brackets and in the color of markers,
 with no data: [DATE], [PB], [KEY].
Short | The short name and the data of the marker: [LN Length:2 in], [PB Odd
 Page], [Key: Enter].  A value marker shows its value: [November 13, 2026].
Long | The whole word and the data: [LINE Length:2 in, Weight:0.01 in],
 [COLUMN BREAK].  A Counter and a Cross-reference show their text.
Hidden | Nothing.  The marker is still there; the cursor stops on it.
Inline | Only for the Equation, the Picture, the Keycap and the LaTeX
 marker, and their default: the image that is made of the marker, in
 the X11 version.  When the image cannot be made, and in the terminal
 version, the marker is shown in the Short form.
.end
.pic pictures/markers-modes-panel.png 5.4 The first of the three pages of the panel Marker Display Modes: the global mode and the mode of each marker.
A marker that is hidden is shown anyway, in the Short form, when Display Hidden
Markers? is Yes in Global, Marker.  A field that is not set is left out of
the label.  The labels are drawn between square brackets; a
Counter and a Cross-reference in the Long mode are drawn as their text without
brackets, in the color of markers.

The table gives the label that each marker has in each mode, for a marker
with the fields shown after the name.
.table 15 13 15 15
Marker (example) | Name | Short | Long
Author | AUTHOR | AUTHOR | AUTHOR
Column Break | CB | CB | COLUMN BREAK
Comment "Check" | COM | COM "Check" | COMMENT "Check"
Counter 3 | C | C3 | the text, 7.
Cross-reference | XREF | XREF 1.1 | the text, 1.1
Date | DATE | the date | the date
Description | DESC | DESC | DESCRIPTION
Else | ELSE | ELSE | ELSE
Endif | ENDIF | ENDIF | ENDIF
Equation x^2 | EQN | EQN x^2 | EQUATION x^2
Figure "My fig" | FIG | FIG "My fig" | FIGURE "My fig"
Filedate | FDATE | FILEDATE | FILEDATE
Filename | FNAME | the name | the name
Filetime | FTIME | FILETIME | FILETIME
Font | FONT | FONT | FONT
If "A = 1" | IF | IF "A = 1" | IF "A = 1"
Include "x.doc" | INCLUDE | INCLUDE "x.doc" | INCLUDE "x.doc"
Index Heading | INDEX | INDEX Heading | INDEX Heading
Keep | KEEP | Start Keep (Fixed) | Start Keep (Fixed)
Keycap Enter | KEY | Key: Enter | Key: Enter
LaTeX | LaTeX | LaTeX instructions | LaTeX instructions
Line | LN | LN Length:RM | LINE Length:RM
Merge "NAME" | MERGE | MERGE "NAME" | MERGE "NAME", Delete:Yes
Note "text" | NOTE | NOTE | NOTE "text"
Orientation | ORIENTATION | ORIENTATION | ORIENTATION
Page Break | PB | PB | PB
Page Number | PAGE | PAGE | PAGE
Pathname | PATH | the path | the path
Picture {c:a.png} | PIC | PIC {c:a.png} | PIC {c:a.png}
Printer | PRINTER | the name | the name
Revision | REV | 1.001 | 1.001
Section | SECTION | SECTION | SECTION
Table "T1" | TABLE | TABLE "T1" | TABLE "T1"
Time | TIME | the time | the time
Whitespace 3 in | WS | WS 3 in | WHITESPACE 3 in, ...
.end
The details that this table cannot hold (the words that a Page Break, a Line,
an Orientation marker, a Keep marker or a Whitespace marker adds to its label)
are in the entries, which give the exact labels.

The next three pictures show one document that holds a marker of each kind, in
front of a copy of its name, in three display modes: each marker in its own
default mode, every marker in the Name mode, and every marker in the Long
mode.
.pic pictures/markers-all-default.png 5.4 A document with a marker of each kind, each in its default display mode.
.pic pictures/markers-all-name.png 5.4 The same document with every marker in the Name mode.
.pic pictures/markers-all-long.png 5.4 The same document with every marker in the Long mode.

.note The Filedate and Filetime markers show their name on the screen, not the
  date, because the date of the file is only known when the file is written.
  The Date and Time markers show the date and time now.  The Filename marker
  shows the name of the document in capitals.

.h2 How a marker is printed [markers-printed]
.ix Printing markers
.ix Markers, printing
.ix Long form of a marker
.ix Boxed label
.ix Draft print and markers
Markers are carried out when the Printer makes the pages: in a Final print, in
a Preview and in a Draft print ({n:print-elements}).  For nearly every marker
the display mode (Diamond, Short, Hidden and so on) does not change what is
printed: a Hidden marker still prints.  The exception is six value markers,
Date, Time, Filename, Pathname, Printer and Revision, which follow the display
mode, as the subsection on value markers below says.
.h3 Four ways to print a marker
.table 10 50
Way | What is printed
Text | The marker is replaced by a text that is made when printing: the author,
 the date, the number of a counter, the tag of a section, the page number, the
 text of the document that an Include marker names.  The
 text is typeset in the font of the text around it.
Typeset | The marker makes something on the page: a rule or a row of
 characters (Line), a vertical space (Whitespace), a page break (Page Break,
 Column Break), a landscape or portrait page (Orientation), a note, a picture,
 an equation, a keycap, a change of the family of type (Font), the
 instructions of a LaTeX marker.
Label | The label of the marker in its long form, printed in a small typewriter
 face in a thin box.  The label shows where the marker stands and what it
 holds ({n:markers-labels}).
Nothing | Nothing is printed.  The Keep marker, and a Comment in a Final print.
.end
The label is the long form of the marker, that is, the text that the Long
display mode gives (a Merge marker is printed as MERGE "NAME", Delete:Yes).  A marker
whose picture or equation cannot be made (a missing file, a file that TeX
cannot read, a formula that TeX does not accept) is also printed as a label.
The
markers that are always printed as a label are Figure, Index, Merge
and Table ({n:markers-labels}).

.h3 Draft and Final
A Final print (and the Preview, which is made the same way) leaves out the
comments and draws the pictures.  A Draft print is a quick print: it prints
the comments (in brackets, with the name of the commenter and the time),
sets the text in single spacing and prints the pictures as a Final print does,
in color.  The
other panels of the Print menu are in {n:print}.

.h3 Value markers and the display mode [markers-print-modes]
.ix Display mode and print
The display mode of the type is applied to the text of the Date, Time,
Filename, Pathname, Printer and Revision markers.  In the modes Diamond,
Short and Long the Printer prints the value.  In the Name mode it prints the
name (DATE, TIME, FNAME, PATH, PRINTER, REV).  In the Hidden mode it prints
nothing.  The other value markers (Author, Description, Filedate and
Filetime) and the Counter and the Cross-reference do not follow the mode and
always print their value.  A date that is set to Name or Hidden to get it out
of the way on the screen is set back before printing.

.h3 Markers in tables and boxes
A Page Break, a Column Break or an Orientation marker cannot take effect
inside the cell of a table or inside a kept region (see Keep); in a table cell
the Page Break and Column Break markers print as labels, and a Keep region
that holds one of these markers or an Orientation marker is not kept: the Keep
markers are dropped and the text is set as if they were not there.  A footnote
inside a table cell waits until the table has ended.

.h3 Limits of the printed markers
- The Unit field of a Picture is used for the picture on the screen; on paper
  the Width and the Height are read as inches.
